{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And he turned.</b> He turned them from the straight road to the winding road." "<b>Sea of Suf.</b> Like 'to the Sea of Suf.' And סוּף is a term for a marsh where reeds grow, as in ״וַתָּשֶׂם בַּסּוּף״ (Exodus 2), ״קָנֶה וָסוּף קָמֵלוּ״ (Isaiah 19)." "<b>And ḥamushim.</b> חֲמֻשִׁים means nothing but armed. (Because he led them roundabout through the wilderness, that is what caused them to go up armed — for had he led them by a settled route, they would not have provisioned themselves with everything they needed, but rather like a person traveling from place to place intending to buy there what he needs; but when one sets out into the wilderness he must provision himself with every need. And this verse was written only to satisfy the ear, so that you not wonder, in the war against Amalek and in the war against Sihon and Og and Midian, where they got the weapons with which they struck them down with the sword.) And so it says, ״וְאַתֶּם תַּעַבְרוּ חֲמֻשִׁים״ (Joshua 1), and so Onkelos renders: מְזָרְזִין, as in ״וַיָּרֶק אֶת חֲנִיכָיו״ (Genesis 14) — and זָרֵיז. Another interpretation: חֲמֻשִׁים — one out of five went out, and four parts died during the three days of darkness (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:01.915305099Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}